nt and American Presidency Project record. ([trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov](https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefings-statements/statement-by-the-president-41/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Imposed targeted Global Magnitsky sanctions on senior Chinese officials (including Xinjiang party chief Chen Quanguo) and the Xinjiang Public Security Bureau for serious human‑rights abuses (July 9, 2020), via OFAC/Treasury. ([home.treasury.gov](https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sm1055?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Established and used Executive Order 13818 (Dec 20, 2017) to combat “serious human rights abuse or corruption” worldwide—the U.S. framework used for many Global Magnitsky designations; see the order and Treasury’s overview. ([presidency.ucsb.edu](https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-13818-blocking-the-property-persons-involved-serious-human-rights-abuse-or?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Signed the Hong Kong Human Rights and Democracy Act (Nov 27, 2019), linking Hong Kong’s special treatment to autonomy/human-rights conditions and authorizing sanctions against perpetrators of abuses; see Congress.gov and the presidential signing statement. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/116th-congress/senate-bill/1838?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Convened a UN event calling for global protection of religious freedom (Sept 23, 2019), urging governments to end persecution and release prisoners of conscience; corroborated by White House and UN News. ([trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov](https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefings-statements/remarks-president-trump-united-nations-event-religious-freedom-new-york-ny/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Met at the White House with survivors of religious persecution from multiple countries (July 17, 2019), highlighting support for victims and religious liberty; see the official transcript and Freedom House’s statement. ([trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov](https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefings-statements/remarks-president-